<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristippus of Cyrene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 435 – c. 356 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristippus of Cyrene was a Greek philosopher and pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> who founded the Cyrenaic school. He taught that the pleasure of the present moment is the highest good, while urging self-control so as to master pleasure rather than be ruled by it. Anecdotes describe him as a worldly man who charged fees for teaching and spent time at the court of Dionysius of Syracuse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristippus-of-cyrene.jpg" alt="Aristippus of Cyrene" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gottlieb Friedrich Riegel (1724–1784) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristippus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aristippus was the son of Aritades and was born at Cyrene around 435 BCE. He is said to have come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> to attend the Olympic games, where he met Ischomachus and asked about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>. Ischomachus's description gave him a strong desire to see Socrates, and he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and made him his master.</p><p>Though a disciple, he departed in principle and in practice from the teaching and example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>. He is also said to have been the first of Socrates's followers to take money for teaching. He sometimes sent it to Socrates, who often returned it, viewing it as an insult. There are indications that Aristippus has been conflated with his grandson, Aristippus the Younger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Aristippus turned toward the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I of Syracuse</a>, or of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-ii-of-syracuse">Dionysius the Younger</a>, where he became a counsellor. Accounts say that he lived luxuriously there and kept the company of Lais. He told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> that he lived abroad to avoid involvement in the politics of his native city. He is also said to have been taken prisoner by Artaphernes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> who drove the Spartans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 396 BCE.</p><p>Vitruvius tells that when Aristippus was shipwrecked on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> he saw geometrical figures drawn on the shore and said, according to the story, that he saw the traces of man. He went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> of Rhodes, discussed philosophy and received gifts. The message he sent home was that children should be given the kind of property that can swim out of a shipwreck. In old age he is said to have returned to Cyrene and to have died at 79.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Aristippus taught that pleasure is the supreme good, a view later called ethical hedonism, but that it should be moderated by self-restraint, and he warned his followers never to harm others. His best-known phrase is I possess, I am not possessed. Diogenes Laertius reports him as saying that the best course is not abstinence from pleasures but mastery over them without ever being worsted.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> accused him of straying from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, who valued virtue above pleasure, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called him a sophist. In Plato's Phaedo he is described as having been at Aegina, a pleasure resort, instead of attending Socrates's death. His daughter Arete, whom he named his intellectual heiress, and her son Aristippus the Younger turned his teaching into the system of the Cyrenaic school.</p><p>None of his works survives. Diogenes Laertius lists books ascribed to him on the authority of Sotion and Panaetius, but notes Sosicrates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>'s statement that he wrote nothing. A book called On Ancient Luxury cannot be his, since its author mentions Theophrastus, who lived a generation later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aristippus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was an intellectual centre: <b>Aristippus</b> founded the Cyrenaic school of philosophy, and the mathematician Theodorus taught there.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cyrene">Republic of Cyrene</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cyrene, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. Died in Cyrene, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.82,21.85&amp;year=-384" aria-label="Cyrene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5110 971 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aristippus of Cyrene"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5560 1241h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5560 1241h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.82,21.85&amp;year=-384">What was here in 384 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If all the laws should be abrogated, we should still live in the same manner as we do now.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 2 &#183; tr. C. D. Yonge, 1853 &#183; asked what advantage philosophers had over other men &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57342/pg57342-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it is not those who know a great many things, but they who know what is useful who are valuable men.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 2 &#183; tr. C. D. Yonge, 1853 &#183; to a man proud of the variety of his learning &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57342/pg57342-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Send them both naked among strangers, and you will find out.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 2 &#183; tr. C. D. Yonge, 1853 &#183; asked in what a wise man is superior to one who is not wise &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57342/pg57342-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
